\dfrac{1}{s+1} &amp; 0\\ \dfrac{1}{(s+1)^2} &amp; \dfrac{1}{s+1}
\end{array}\right]\] Taking inverse Laplace transform element-wise, \[\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{1}{s+1}
\right\}
=
e^{-t}\] \[\mathcal{L}^{-1}
\left\{
\frac{1}{(s+1)^2}
\right\}
=
te^{-t}\] Hence, \[e^{At}
=
